me value stored at a different page (maybe \"Player_XSpeed\" is $0057, and $0700 is \"Player_XSpeed\" too?).\n\nHmm wait. Let me reconsider SMB memory layout. I now recall more concretely from the SMB RAM map:\n```\n$0057 - Player's horizontal speed? \n$0700 - Player's horizontal speed? \n```\nActually I think I recall: `Player_XSpeed = $0057` and `Player_Y_Speed = $00F0`? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the object variable layout: In SMB, the player is object 0. The object arrays start at $0016 (Enemy_ID), $0087 (Enemy_X), $00CF (Enemy_Y)... and speeds might be at $0057 and $00? Hmm, $001D? \n\nWait, our watch earlier of 0x19-0x28 showed all zeros, but that was during walking right — if speed were there it'd be nonzero. So no.\n\nLet me check other plausible speed addresses. Actually SMB object \"X speed\" arrays: maybe $0057? and \"Y speed\" $00F0? Hmm.\n\nLet me now find vertical speed (jump velocity). Approach: pause (emulator) and create a state where Mario is rising or falling with a known vertical velocity. Using the same Start-pause trick: jump (A) then pause mid-jump? But pressing Start mid-air pauses the game; vertical velocity would be retained. Then diff against a grounded state.\n\nBut actually, for vertical speed, a clean differential: \n- v_ground: Mario standing on ground (y velocity 0).\n- v_air: Mario mid-jump (y velocity negative = rising, or positive = falling).\n\nWe can capture v_air by: unpause, jump (hold A), then press Start at a chosen frame mid-jump to pause. Then snapshot. But the timing of when to press Start determines whether he's rising or falling. We can choose: after A for ~10 frames, press Start (mid-rise). Then velocity is negative (upward). Or after A release, wait, then Start (falling, positive).\n\nHmm, but velocity might be small. Let's think about what values to expect. SMB jump velocity: initial upward speed ~ -0x28? Actually gravity/jump: Mario's vertical speed for a jump is around 0x27 or so with subpixel? Might be stored as magnitude with a separate \"falling\" flag again.\n\nBut wait — the existing mapping note says y position at $00CE, and a copy at $03B8. There may be a Y speed array analogous.\n\nLet me search for it via differential: use the Start-pause trick to freeze a falling state vs grounded.\n\nPlan:\n- v3: standing on ground, paused (we can create it: unpause, wait for Mario to land and stand, pause). Actually currently v2 is paused mid-walk-left, Mario grounded. Velocity horizontal 21, vertical 0 (grounded). Good, we can use v2 as \"grounded\" but it has horizontal speed too. For vertical speed isolation, compare same horizontal? Hmm.\n\nBetter: create a grounded standing paused state v3: unpause, no input, wait ~20 frames (velocity decays, stands), pause. Then create mid-air paused state v4: unpause, jump, pause at ascent. Then diff v3->v4. Vertical velocity changes 0 -> negative (up). Horizontal may also change if jump has no side input. With no directional input, horizontal speed stays 0. Good. So the diff isolates vertical velocity + y position + animation.\n\nBut note: y velocity sign. If stored signed, rising = negative (large unsigned) -> filter changed/decreased. If magnitude+direction flag, then it's positive.\n\nLet's first find y velocity candidates. Do:\nv3 = grounded paused, standing.\nv4 = paused mid-jump (rising).\ndiff v3->v4 changed, filter to find.\n\nBut there will be lots of changes (y pos, OAM, animation). We can narrow.\n\nActually, we already have v0 (grounded standing paused, from before). But state changed since. Let's make fresh.\n\nActually, better approach: use trace_writes on the candidate horizontal speed address to confirm, and use a broader method for y speed.\n\nLet me first nail horizontal speed and direction, since those are half the task.\n\nHorizontal speed: 0x0057 (and 0x0700). Let's confirm which one is canonical by trace_writes while walking. Actually, we can also just verify by writing: write 0x0057 = a big value while paused then step frames and see if Mario moves. But the task says for values rewritten every frame, prove by watch. 0x0057 is likely rewritten every frame by physics; a write would be overwritten. Let's test: while paused (game internally paused), the physics doesn't run, so writing 0x0057 then step_frame (which runs one frame with game paused) — the game is paused so physics skipped, no movement. Hmm.\n\nBetter: use play_input watch to show 0x0057 follows the action. E.g. watch 0x0057 and 0x0700, and 0x0005? while a script does: idle, right, idle (decelerate), left. The samples should show 0 -> ramp to 24 -> decay to 0 -> ramp left 21. That's the proof.\n\nDirection/facing: need a separate address. Let's find it. The direction he faces: pressing Left turns him around. It's likely a byte that is 0/1 (or 1/2). Let's differential: state facing right (v1 walking right paused) vs facing left (v2 walking left paused). diff v1->v2 \"changed\" and look for small byte changing 0<->1. In vd2 (increased) we saw 0x06FC (buttons 1->2? no, 0x06FC = controller; 1=right, 2=left, so that's input not facing). 0x0045 (0->1 in vd1) might be interesting: in v1 it was 1; is it 0 in v2? Let's check. Also 0x070D was 1 in v1.\n\nFacing direction candidate: In vd1, 0x0045 old 0 -> new 1 (walking right). If when walking left it goes back to 0, it's a direction bit. But it could also be animation.\n\nLet's do diff v1->v2 filter changed from vd1 and inspect the small values. Actually let me just do diff v0->v2 \"changed\"? Hmm.\n\nBetter: diff v1 (right) vs v2 (left) with filter changed, no from, and look for addresses with values 0/1 or 1/2. But 2048 range.\n\nLet me do diff v1->v2 filter changed and look. But many changes. Let's instead use filter \"decreased\" from vd1: addresses that increased 0->something in vd1 and then decreased back. 0x0045 (0->1) if decreases to 0 would show. Let's run diff v1->v2 filter decreased from vd1.\n\nActually we already have vd2 (increased from vd1 survivors) = 22 survivor