hould show left-facing if facing byte is 0.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: 0x070D might be \"moving right flag\" (like the button direction) rather than facing. But it persisted while standing (0x070D=1 in rface, =0 in lface, both standing). So it's persistent = facing. Good.\n\nWait, but 0x0045 and 0x0033 also differ (1 vs 2). Those might be animation frames or \"PlayerFacingDir\" with values 1/2. Hmm. Let's check: 0x070D 0/1; 0x0033 2/1; 0x0045 2/1.\n\nActually in SMB, the player's facing direction might be at $0033? Hmm. Let's just test writes.\n\nLet me test 0x070D first. Save state before writing (save_state) so I can rollback.\n\nActually, writes to RAM are easily undone by writing back. Since paused, I can just write the old value back. But save_state is safer.\n\nLet me: save_state slot 0 (currently rface paused). write 0x070D=0. Then run frames to redraw.\n\nActually simpler proof that doesn't depend on pause engine: write the byte, then play_input no-buttons few frames. Let me do it.\n\nBut I should isolate: if I write 0x070D=0 and Mario flips, that's proof. If not, try 0x0033 or 0x0045.\n\nLet me also get a baseline screenshot of rface (facing right). Actually Mario is small Mario, facing right. Let me screenshot now."}
