t_sprites → right baseline (record OAM attr bytes).\n3. run left 40 frames, stop 15, pause, list_sprites → left.\n\nLet me just do it and compare.\n\nBut honestly, maybe the simplest: the direction he faces in SMB is stored at $000D I think? Let me just brute-force test writes for the candidate bytes and watch flip_h.\n\nCandidates in lface vs rface with different values: 0x0000 (0 vs 2), 0x0003 (2 vs 1), 0x0033 (2 vs 1), 0x0045 (2 vs 1), 0x070D (0 vs 1).\n\nWe tested 0x0033→2 (no flip) and 0x070D→0 (no flip). Remaining: 0x0000, 0x0003, 0x0045.\n\nActually hold on: maybe in lface the value is 2 and rface 1 for 0x0033 and 0x0045; testing 0x0033=2 didn't flip. Maybe the correct one is 0x0045. Let's test it.\n\nBut wait, actually maybe the facing byte doesn't control flip_h at all for small Mario; maybe small Mario's left-facing uses tiles like $3A/$4F with flip. Let me actually look at read_tile for the current sprites vs expected.\n\nHmm, let me look at the OAM attr directly. list_sprites \"attr\":0 for Mario sprites. For left-facing, attr would be 0x40 (h-flip). Let me get a true left-facing via more input.\n\nLet me load rface, run left 30, stop, list.\n\nActually, I realize the issue with my earlier test: after holding left 8 frames then releasing and waiting 15, Mario comes to a stop. When stopping, SMB's sprite routine may show the \"stand\" pose. But tiles 50-53 identical to right... \n\nLet me just compare directly. Load state 0, list (right). Then left 30, list (left). If identical, something's off with how the sprite direction works in this ROM.\n\nLet me do it."}
